\begin_layout Section
Contribution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This thesis introduces the principle of familiarity, by means of which solutions
 are characterized as interpretable the less they deviate from the expert's
 solution.
 To our knowledge, familiarity has not yet been used as a reference for
 better understanding.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For this purpose, the complexity measure of genetic programming, which is
 based on the number of nodes of the programming tree, is calculated as
 the number of necessary changes to the expert's programming tree.
 This is implemented with the help of the tree edit distance, which is already
 known as a problem in theoretical computer science.
 Thus, GP still tries to achieve the same goal, but would be more bound
 to the heuristic given by the expert.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We use transition samples of a non-specific black box policy as a proxy
 model to let genetic programming elaborate the main differences to a domain
 expert's program.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Outline
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The structure of this thesis is organized as follows.
 Chapter xx2 provides background information covering the most important
 aspects of interpretability in the context of reinforcement learning.
 Chapter xx3 introduces the concept of familiarity and discribes the implementat
ion in the form of the tree edit distance as second order optimization.
 Fixed nodes are further introduced as logical extension to some shortcomings
 of the edit distance.
 The approach is evaluated using symbolic regression on transition samples
