\begin_layout Section
Genetic Programming 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "gp_Genetic-Programming"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Genetic programming (GP) is an evolutionary algorithm that can automatically
 create computer programs.
 It has become a well known approach for creating interpretable system controlli
ng policies since it was first introduced by Koza 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Koza_GP_patent_1990"
literal "true"

\end_inset

.
 GP is an evolutionary machine learning technique that can automatically
 create programs through evolutionary methods.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It requires two essential things the representation of programs as combination
 of genes a fitness function to rate individuals of a population.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Tree Construction 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The representation as computational trees allows for an efficient evolution
 procedure.
 Each tree generated must be evaluable, which is also known as closure.
 Type conversion between boolean and numeric values can be applied automatically
 or with strong typing, which requires explicit type conversion (e.g.
 with comparison operators, if-statements) 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Strongly_typed_GP"
literal "true"

\end_inset

.
 We argue that, even though it is implementation-heavy, strong taped formulas
 are depict a more practicable composition of mathematical components.
 Evaluation safety poses a particular problem, as type-correct expressions
 can be mathematically invalid.
 Frequently occurring functions, e.g.
 the division-function, (exit a division by zero with the dummy value 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

$1$
\end_layout

\end_inset

).
 To avoid changing the essence of other operators too much, faulty trees
 are discarded and replaced randomly.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Evolving a population
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The evolution of a population is a metaheuristic optimization, which means
 that improvements occur stochastically.
 The genepool optimizes through selection and reproduction the best candidates
 between generations; mutation and recombination should improve selection
 even further.
 Whatsoever, the evolutions rely on randomness and do not converge to an
 optimum.
\end_layout

