\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
text{MAE} & = & 
\backslash
frac{1}{n}
\backslash
sum_{i=1}^{n}(y_{i}-
\backslash
hat{y}_{i})
\backslash

\backslash
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
text{MSE} & = & 
\backslash
frac{1}{n}
\backslash
sum_{i=1}^{n}(y_{i}-
\backslash
hat{y}_{i})^{2} 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{eqnarray*}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Both the mean average error (MAE) and the mean square error (MSE) are regression
 metrics (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:regression-error"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

).
 The MAE indicates the average deviation from the target solution and is
 distance-true.
 The MSE squares the distance and thus is xxx not distance-true, making
 it a less honest indicator.
 The MSE, however, severely punishes outliers, lumps of strong deviation
 and volatile solutions, which can lead to a better overall generalisation.
\end_layout

