\begin_layout Paragraph
Implementation
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:depth-based-gp-implementation"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Fixed nodes are unaffected by the evolution process and can be determined,
 starting from the root node down to the leaf nodes for a contiguous real
 subset.
 All trees, including random tree constructions, are required to build on
 this structure.
 In order to continue to use classical GP evolution with the same paradigms,
 some procedures must be re-implemented [xx how?].
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
At least one junction must be offered, but the depth, the size of the succeeding
 branch and the number of branches must be considered for evolution.
 The node depth, which normally indicates the distance to the tree root,
 also loses its meaning for evolutions, why it was replaced with a layer-based
 interpretation, in which the distance of a node to the next fixed node
 is used.
 Due to the high branching factor in trees with several GP interfaces, the
 layer model also loses its function, which is why the number of nodes replaces
 the tree depth as a complexity limit and complexity target in random tree
 generation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Nodesize-based build options including the full- and grow-method.
 Randomly generated trees should cover the entire search space and have
 the structuring Full/Grow options to have the same properties of non-fixed
 generated trees.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Node-count as random trees restriction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Constructing random trees and branches is usually based on the tree depth.
 However, this is a problem for layer-based mutation which is needed with
 fixed nodes, where several branches have to be built up at the same time.
 The tree size can grow exponentially, the starting depth may differ between
 branches and the trees may end up very unbalanced.
 Therefore a node based algorithm was developed, which builds up branches
 in full- and grow mode up to a certain number of nodes without risk of
 structural imbalances.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In order not to violate the principles of GP, xxx.
 At the beginning a random number of nodes to be inserted is chosen.
 Instead of the node depth, layers are defined, which are the distance to
 the next fixed node.
 Depending on the number of nodes located on a layer, the number of nodes
 to be inserted is randomly divided into intervals.
 These subtrees are divided according to the grow or full method up to the
 interval size they are entitled to.
 The number of nodes must be exactly reached (tolerance -1), otherwise the
 trees could have a large variance of nodes per branch.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When building the branches, a buffer of leaf nodes is built up to avoid
 having to prune the tree at the end.
 To build one level lower, a random node of the level is selected and assigned
 according to the Grow or Full method.
 This avoids an overload of the left side, which would occur because only
 limited operators can be used.
\end_layout

