\begin_layout Subsection
Industrial benchmark summary
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Normal GP candidates had no real advantages over the familiar ones, despite
 their enhanced familiarity.
 The regression error and the fitness of both did not distinguish significantly.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The reasons could be with the PSONN policy (the black box), which is too
 different at the beginning of each expert policy tested to allow for any
 hope of improvement.
 Knowledge about the strategy that PSONN was pursuing could therefore not
 be gained - at best, it is a complex approach that can hardly be represented
 by simple formulas.
 All GP races were too similar in their real fitness and safety to allow
 conclusions and fell far short of results trained on the real benchmark.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By combining three different agents, the first combinations with often large
 differences should be ignored.
 Nevertheless, the regression error did not drop low enough in any run to
 explain the data adequately.
 For runs with the squared error, the limit was about 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

$2$
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and average error runs about 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

$1.5$
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 Compared to candidates in the MC benchmark, this is worse by a factor of
 5.
 There can be many reasons for this poor performance, the most likely being
 that the PSONN policy is too complex as a black box to be sufficiently
 approximated by genetic programming.
 This is also confirmed by the results found by Hein in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "daniel_hein_doktorarbeit"
literal "true"

\end_inset

, where the regression error also did not fall significantly below a limit
 of 2.
 Since the candidates cannot expect any performance gain, their evaluation
 would not be meaningful.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
