\begin_layout Section
Conclusion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this thesis, the problem of how a human can learn from black box reinforcemen
t learning policy without having to understand a whole new approach is discussed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Both domain experts and machines can solve reinforcement learning problems.
 The expert can trust his own strategy, but is willing to learn from the
 machine.
 Simple, interpretable control strategies can already be derived from state
 transition samples with genetic programming.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We present the novel concept of familiarity, which depicts similarity to
 prior known strategies.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The implementation is done using the tree edit distance, which calculates
 the necessary changes to an expert programme.
 Programmierbaum berechnet and has the task of replacing simplicity as second
 objective to genetic programming.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Fammas the necessary edits that have to be applied to the domain experts
 origin program.
 In the field of genetic programming, tree edit distance, which has already
 been intensively researched in theoretical computer science, is suitable
 for this purpose.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Reacting to the observation that the TED can loose its protective effect
 partially with simple expert programs (3-6 nodes), we proposed fixed core
 nodes, which allow the expert to define a non-evolutionizable program core.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In order to realise this and other ideas, a GP framework was implemented,
 which extends already known evolution techniques with additional functions
 such as tree unification, reproduction from the Pareto front and fixed
 core structures with the accompanying additional implementations.
 Tensorflow was used for a GPU-enabled parallel evaluation of the regression
 error.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The performance was evaluated on the mountain car and the industrial benchmark
 through symbolic regression on transition samples from neuropolicies and
 various origin programs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
=== Applicability
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Whether a program can be improved depends, on how big the difference is
 to the neuropolicy at the beginning and at the end.If the error is too great,
 the two strategies differ too fundamentally for them to converge in any
 meaningful way.
 The same applies if the regression error does not fall to a certain level.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Problems with smooth system dynamics and convergent solutions are particularly
 suitable.
 The industrial benchmark features a sparse state space and the particle
 swarm optimization neuropolicy is very volatile, which is why no particular
 good solutions were found.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
=== Auswertung
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The results have shown that the candidates in familiar runs structurally
 differed significantly from regular candidates with comparable regression
 errors.
 Familiar structures could be recognized, with the exception of very simple
 expert programs (3-6 nodes), Fixed nodes enabled runs were recognizable
 even with complex candidates and was more robust against misruns.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The real rewards of the mountain car were not only robust against misruns,
 but often exceeded the target solution.
 In order to obtain particularly secure solutions, there are also possibilities
 for expansion here, which, for example, evaluate the candidates additionally
 based on their deviation from the start policy.
\end_layout

