\begin_layout Subsection
Weighted familiar edits
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The TED offers many extension possibilities which can further address the
 nature of a change, including a weighting of nodes similar to the one used
 in classical GP which was mentioned in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:EUREKA-GP-complexities"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For this, the weighted mapping has to be calculated.
 A subsequent weighting of the mapping of the regular TED does not make
 sense, since a later reweighting can lead to ambiguous results that do
 not correspond to the minimum.
 This mainly concerns the handling of a changed node.
 The weighted TED was implemented, but opens many questions on how to deal
 with the effect and the complexity of altered nodes in the origin tree.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Implementations might be based on the following ideas:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Ignoring altered nodes and apply the absolute weighting
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Dividing all operators into classes, where the cost for changing within
 a class is weakened.
 A potential implementation are the operator families (e.g.
 angle-based operators 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

$
\backslash
sin, 
\backslash
cos, 
\backslash
tan$
\end_layout

\end_inset

).
 This allows adaptions in constant nodes and also a distinction between
 basic operator types.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
More specific options that were implemented but not evaluated in order not
 to mix too many paradigms:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
With if-statements, the edit distance of the child branches is calculated
 separately and only the largest factor is added.
 This procedure is intended to promote the use of if-statements, which have
 a very positive influence on the interpretability of a formula due to their
 structuring influence, but which greatly increase the TED due to the high
 arity of 3 and the resulting branching factor.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Use of several known origins in one run and counting the minimum TED for
 each tree.
 This way, several understandable approaches could be compared directly
 for their extensibility or even incomplete solutions could be introduced
 separately.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Unifying and ordering trees before calculating the TED, as can be seen in
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:sympy-ordered-expression"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Similar to fixed nodes (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "subsec:Fixed-nodes"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

), giving nodes of the core structure a separate weighting so that changes
 are possible but very costly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Anomalies with node composition related complexity measures
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As mentioned in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "interpretability-measure"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, the number of nodes poses some problems as a paradigm for complexity values,
 even after weighting.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An important distinction has to be made between the inner and outer nodes
 of a tree.
 The inner nodes, which contain the operators, essentially characterise
 a programming tree by representing the computation that is performed., since
 the exact choice of leaf nodes are the logical prerequisite for its fitness
 and can therefore only be used in the appropriate places.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Operators 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Operators represent the applied computations of a tree and have the most
 debatable implications for interpretability.
 Not only is the total set of operators used of interest, explicability
 depends above all on the structure and type of nesting.
 Examples of this are: xxx chunks here?
\end_layout

